00:01:33.827 --> 00:01:36.160
SAM LUGANI: Well, if you
respond to the attacker's email

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.360
or click on a link
in the email, you

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.864
may end up giving the attacker
access to sensitive data.

00:01:40.864 --> 00:01:41.780
Let's try another one.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:45.119
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:47.480
Well done.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:48.813
You've spotted a phishing email.

00:01:48.813 --> 00:01:49.800
How could you tell?

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.620
YUFENG GUO: Well, this URL
domain name looked suspicious.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:53.370
SAM LUGANI: Right.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:54.000
Good spot.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.770
Details like this
are easy to spot

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.180
if you're looking for them.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.559
But as you've seen, when
you're going through your inbox

00:01:59.559 --> 00:02:01.350
and trying to work,
you don't have the time

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.040
to look at these little
details in emails.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:04.800
YUFENG GUO: Right.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.170
And I can't train
all my employees

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:11.250
to go through each email in
detail to spot phishing emails.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.900
SAM LUGANI: You just got one of
two right, which isn't too bad.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.590
But a report from Intel
Security on phishing attacks

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.950
showed that 97% of users
are not able to identify

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.020
a sophisticated phishing attack.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.490
But luckily, if you're
a security admin,

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.640
you have a few tools
at your disposal.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.350
The first tool is Gmail.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.530
Gmail uses the power of a
billion plus user community

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.570
to protect against
phishing attacks.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.994
As emails come into
your Gmail inbox,

00:02:35.994 --> 00:02:37.410
they will automatically
be scanned

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.230
to identify phishing emails.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:40.920
And if a phishing
email is detected,

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.660
it's discarded before
hitting the user's inbox.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.920
But these emails are often very
sophisticated and difficult

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.000
to spot.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.690
So Gmail now uses machine
learning to identify patterns

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.370
across messages.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:56.740
This allows Gmail to
identify suspicious emails

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.970
that the ML engine says have
a high probability of being

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:00.864
a phishing email.

00:03:00.864 --> 00:03:01.530
YUFENG GUO: Wow.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.260
So with machine learning,
Gmail is finding patterns

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:05.970
in phishing emails
from billions of users

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.750
and using that ML model
to identify emails

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:10.809
with a high phishing
score or prediction.

00:03:10.809 --> 00:03:11.850
SAM LUGANI: Yes, exactly.

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:14.400
So as attackers launch
more phishing attacks,

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.080
Gmail is learning
from those attacks

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.379
and getting smarter at
defending against them.

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:20.670
YUFENG GUO: So with Gmail,
most phishing attacks either

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.920
don't hit my employee
inboxes at all

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.540
or these emails are sent with a
warning if they're suspicious.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:26.790
SAM LUGANI: Yes, that's right.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.680
Gmail protects your
employees from having

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.080
to worry about scanning
each email manually

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.430
for phishing attacks.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:33.960
YUFENG GUO: So is
Gmail all I need

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:35.675
to stop these phishing attacks?

00:03:35.675 --> 00:03:37.050
SAM LUGANI: Gmail
is a first line

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.450
of defense and essential for
preventing phishing email

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:40.134
attacks.

00:03:40.134 --> 00:03:41.550
But you also need
a way to protect

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.460
against phishing websites
and stolen passwords, which

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.160
is why Gmail works hand-in-hand
with Chrome browser

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.020
and tighten security
keys to provide end

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.230
to end phishing protection
for your company.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.690
YUFENG GUO: Well, Sam, thank
you so much for showing us

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.200
how companies can defend against
these attacks with Google

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:58.730
Cloud.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:03:59.730
SAM LUGANI: My pleasure.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:02.830
